      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.069,
            0.816,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "111"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.108,
            0.725,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "2. Let A and B be two points on a circle with centre O."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.135,
            0.883,
            0.155
          ],
          "content": "(i) Are there points X, Y on the circle, on the same side of AB,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.157,
            0.661,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "such that ∠AXB is different from ∠AYB?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.182,
            0.661,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Is it true that if $\\angle AXB = \\angle AHB$ , then X and Y lie on the same side of the circle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.229,
            0.661,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "(iii) If $\\angle AXB = \\angle AHB$ , and X and Y do not lie on the circle, does the circle through A, B and X also pass through Y?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.671,
            0.159,
            0.884,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.301,
            0.37,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": "3. Find x in Fig. 5.26."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.745,
            0.322,
            0.822,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 5.26"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.338,
            0.492,
            0.36
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.8 Concyclicity of Points"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.367,
            0.885,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "Now we address the question about when 4 points lie on the same circle. And we will let you explore the question for 5 points, 6 points and so on! We call points that are on the same circle **concyclic**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.429,
            0.886,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 10:** If a line segment AB joining two points A, B subtends equal angles at two other points C, D that lie on the same side of AB, then the four points lie on a circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.491,
            0.543,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "As always we draw the picture first."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.514,
            0.886,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": "**Given:** Consider segment AB. Points C and D lie on the same side of AB; points C and D are not on the line AB; and $\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.557,
            0.599,
            0.577
          ],
          "content": "**To show:** A, B, C, D lie on the same circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.581,
            0.883,
            0.622
          ],
          "content": "**Why is this true?** The points A, B, C are noncollinear points. So, using Theorem 1, there is a circle passing through A, B, C."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.625,
            0.884,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "Let us show that D also lies on that circle. For this, let us draw the circle through A, B and C."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.667,
            0.884,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "Suppose D is not on the circle; then it is either inside or outside the circle. Join AD."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.711,
            0.418,
            0.897
          ],
